    body: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Bluebonnet season is not a single weekend circled on a Texas calendar. It is a moving, weather-dependent bloom that begins earlier in warmer parts of the state and generally works north as spring advances. Rainfall months before the flowers appear, winter temperatures, soil moisture, mowing schedules and local exposure all influence what a roadside looks like in March or April. The practical lesson is simple: plan a route, not one guaranteed field.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'TxDOT’s Wildflower Program manages roadside vegetation with native grasses and wildflowers in mind and says more than 5,000 wildflower species grow along Texas roadsides. Bluebonnets are only the most famous part of that system. A strong spring drive may include Indian paintbrush, phlox, coreopsis, verbena and other species mixed into the same right of way, and the best-looking stretch can change from one week to the next.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'When bluebonnets usually bloom' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'TxDOT notes that bluebonnet flowering commonly starts around mid-March in southern parts of Texas while northern areas can be later, and that an individual flowering period is roughly a month. In practice, Central Texas and the Hill Country often draw the largest crowds from late March into April, but there is no statewide “peak day.” A cold snap can slow one region while a warm, wet stretch pushes another ahead.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Treat social-media reports as observations, not forecasts. A photo posted three days ago may already describe a different field, especially after heavy rain, wind, mowing or a hot spell. Local visitor bureaus, parks, wildflower centers and recent community reports can help, but build enough flexibility into the day that a missed field does not ruin the trip.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Where to build a bluebonnet drive' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Ennis is famous for mapped spring drives through the Blackland Prairie, while Washington County and the Brenham area are long-running wildflower destinations east of Austin. In the Hill Country, roads around Llano, Burnet, Marble Falls, Fredericksburg and smaller ranch communities can produce dramatic displays when rainfall and timing cooperate. The point is not that every named road blooms every year; it is that these areas give you enough connected countryside to keep exploring.' },
      { type: 'list', items: [
        'Choose a loop with several towns, parks or historic stops so the day still works if the flowers are weak.',
        'Leave early enough that you are not trying to stop on a crowded shoulder at midday.',
        'Use public parks, designated pullouts and legitimate parking areas whenever possible.',
        'Never treat a fence opening, ranch gate or private drive as an invitation to enter a field.',
        'Have a second route in mind. Wildflowers reward wandering more than certainty.'
      ] },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'The roadside safety rule matters more than the picture' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The most dangerous bluebonnet behavior has nothing to do with flowers. It is stopping a vehicle where other drivers do not expect it. TxDOT encourages people to enjoy roadside wildflowers but warns against damaging them and stresses safe roadside behavior. Do not leave a vehicle partly in a travel lane, stop on a blind curve, block a ranch entrance or send children across a highway because the better patch is on the opposite shoulder.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'A safe location may not produce the most dramatic photograph, and that is fine. The field will not look better because traffic is moving three feet behind you. Public parks, county roads with genuinely safe pull-offs, visitor centers, wildflower centers and community-managed trails are better places to spend time than a narrow highway shoulder.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Do not flatten the flowers for the photograph' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'TxDOT specifically discourages picture-taking that damages wildflowers because annual plants need to mature and set seed. The easiest way to protect a patch is to photograph from its edge, use a longer lens or lower camera angle and keep groups on existing paths or already-open ground. A family does not need to sit in the center of the densest flowers to make the scene look full.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The old argument over whether picking a bluebonnet is itself illegal misses the more useful point. Roadside access, traffic laws, private property rules, park rules and protection of particular land can all matter. Even where picking a common flower is not separately prohibited, trampling a roadside patch or trespassing for a photograph is still poor stewardship.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'A better bluebonnet photo plan' },
      { type: 'list', items: [
        'Photograph early or late when light is lower and traffic can be easier to manage.',
        'Shoot from the edge of the patch and let perspective make the flowers look deeper.',
        'Keep children and pets on the traffic-safe side of the scene.',
        'Watch for fire ants, snakes, uneven ground and roadside debris before sitting down.',
        'Do not move signs, climb fences or open gates for a cleaner background.',
        'If a place is crowded enough that everyone is inventing a parking space, keep driving.'
      ] },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Planting your own is a fall project' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The biggest home-garden mistake is buying bluebonnet seed while the state is blooming and expecting the same show immediately. TxDOT’s planting guidance says most Texas wildflowers, especially bluebonnets, are best planted in late summer or fall. Seed needs good soil contact, appropriate drainage and the right exposure, and some seed can take far longer than one season to germinate.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Bluebonnets prefer well-drained conditions and plenty of sun. TxDOT advises shallow coverage—no more than about a quarter inch—and warns that heavy fertilization can produce more foliage instead of more flowers. The correct seed mix also varies by region. What thrives on calcareous Hill Country soil is not automatically the best choice for a shaded East Texas yard or a wet Gulf Coast site.' },
      { type: 'shop', collectionSlug: 'wildflower-house' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Make the drive about Texas, not just one flower' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The best bluebonnet trips have a second reason to exist: a courthouse square, a state park, a barbecue lunch, a historic site, a short hike, a bakery, a river crossing or a town you have never stopped in. That makes the trip resilient when the bloom is early, late or patchy, and it spreads visitors across more than one over-photographed roadside.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Spring is one of the few times when ordinary Texas infrastructure becomes part of the attraction. Farm roads, fence lines, highway rights of way and vacant corners of public land turn into a state-scale garden. The responsible way to enjoy it is to move slowly, park carefully, stay where you are allowed, leave the plants standing and accept that the best field may be the one nobody promised you would find.' },
    ],
